1
00:00:01,028 --> 00:00:03,863
Phase Shift is a puzzle
game about interference:

2
00:00:04,053 --> 00:00:06,993
what makes a quantum computer
more than a coin flip.

3
00:00:07,143 --> 00:00:13,175
It runs on Qollab: press Run, pick a
simulator, and Qiskit starts in your browser.

4
00:00:13,611 --> 00:00:15,364
Here's the only rule you need.

5
00:00:16,051 --> 00:00:17,853
Every path carries an arrow.

6
00:00:18,035 --> 00:00:21,425
Where paths meet, the
arrows add head to tail.

7
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:24,038
Lined up, a detector lights up.

8
00:00:24,196 --> 00:00:26,994
Opposite, they cancel: it goes dark.

9
00:00:27,541 --> 00:00:29,904
Level 1, Invisible Ink.

10
00:00:30,084 --> 00:00:33,207
A Hadamard splits the
qubit into two paths;

11
00:00:33,288 --> 00:00:35,763
a phase turns the lower path's arrow.

12
00:00:35,949 --> 00:00:38,782
I run it at 0°: 50/50.

13
00:00:38,956 --> 00:00:42,770
I turn it to 180° and
run again: still 50/50.

14
00:00:42,948 --> 00:00:44,527
The phase is invisible:

15
00:00:44,646 --> 00:00:47,631
measuring sees an arrow's
length, not its direction.

16
00:00:47,781 --> 00:00:50,803
I switch on a second
Hadamard so the paths meet.

17
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:54,926
Same phase, run: 100%.

18
00:00:55,125 --> 00:00:57,305
Why? Follow the arrows.

19
00:00:57,478 --> 00:00:59,932
Classical rules give 50%;

20
00:01:00,226 --> 00:01:02,404
quantum rules give 100%.

21
00:01:02,576 --> 00:01:06,833
That difference is interference,
and no coin flip can reach it.

22
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:09,474
Dark Port hides a phase:

23
00:01:09,514 --> 00:01:11,890
brightness tells you how
far you are from dark.

24
00:01:12,154 --> 00:01:15,957
In the Eraser, a spying detector
kills the interference.

25
00:01:16,138 --> 00:01:20,912
Erase what it knows, and interference
returns, but only in the correlations.

26
00:01:21,335 --> 00:01:24,058
New: level 4, Kickback.

27
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:28,283
A controlled phase acts only
when both qubits are 1.

28
00:01:28,495 --> 00:01:31,641
With the target at 0, the phase vanishes.

29
00:01:31,812 --> 00:01:35,576
Flip the target with an X, and the
phase kicks back onto the control:

30
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:38,621
it reads 1, while the target stays 1.

31
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:42,140
That's the trick behind phase
estimation and Shor's algorithm.

32
00:01:42,562 --> 00:01:48,181
Also new: level 5, Amplifier,
a 2-qubit Grover search.

33
00:01:48,846 --> 00:01:50,970
The prize is behind door 11.

34
00:01:51,132 --> 00:01:52,544
I mark it with a phase.

35
00:01:52,687 --> 00:01:56,081
On its own, the mark is
invisible: 25% each.

36
00:01:56,387 --> 00:02:02,400
The diffuser reflects every arrow
about the average. One query: 100%.

37
00:02:02,599 --> 00:02:04,742
Four arrows line up on the prize.

38
00:02:05,164 --> 00:02:09,920
Then Run on QPU sends this exact circuit
to the backend you chose in Qollab,

39
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:11,036
as one job.

40
00:02:11,454 --> 00:02:13,947
Here that's Qollab's built-in simulator.

41
00:02:14,097 --> 00:02:19,806
Pick IonQ's simulator in Select QPU, and
the same button earns the IonQ star.

42
00:02:20,498 --> 00:02:22,977
On a phone, Run stays under your thumb.

43
00:02:23,111 --> 00:02:25,390
It's keyboard and
screen-reader accessible:

44
00:02:25,687 --> 00:02:29,400
the histogram is a table, and
every arrow is described in text.

45
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:37,010
It's real Qiskit, MIT licensed, with 207
tests, and every level is checked for IonQ.

46
00:02:37,264 --> 00:02:42,377
To add a puzzle, write one Level in
levels.py and one feedback function.

47
00:02:42,561 --> 00:02:44,324
The interface builds itself.

48
00:02:44,774 --> 00:02:45,841
Phase Shift.

49
00:02:46,034 --> 00:02:50,662
Five puzzles, one rule: follow
the arrows. Play it on Qollab.

